## 3.2. Rewrite PersianScheduleParser

Fix and thoroughly test the Persian schedule parser.

It must correctly understand:

- Persian digits.
- Arabic digits.
- English digits.
- Persian number words.
- Common conversational Persian expressions.
- Persian date expressions.
- Persian time expressions.
- Morning, afternoon, evening, and midnight indicators.

Correctly support these examples:

| Persian command | Expected time |
|---|---|
| ساعت ۷ صبح | 07:00 |
| ساعت ۷ عصر | 19:00 |
| ساعت ۷:۳۰ عصر | 19:30 |
| ساعت ۱۲ ظهر | 12:00 |
| ساعت ۱۲ شب | 00:00 |
| ساعت ۱۲:۳۰ شب | 00:30 |
| ساعت ۱۲:۳۰ ظهر | 12:30 |
| ساعت ۱۸:۴۵ | 18:45 |
| ساعت ۹ و نیم صبح | 09:30 |
| نیم ساعت دیگر | 30 minutes from now |
| یک ربع دیگر | 15 minutes from now |

Also understand expressions such as:

- فردا
- پس‌فردا
- امروز
- آخر هفته
- هفته آینده
- شنبه آینده
- سه‌شنبه ساعت ۱۰
- ده دقیقه قبل از جلسه
- یک ربع به ۹
- نیم ساعت بعد از ظهر

Important corrections:

- Never convert 7 PM into 7 AM.
- Never convert midnight into noon.
- Never remove meaningful parts of task titles.
- Do not remove the standalone Persian word «کار» when cleaning task titles.
- Use token-aware text normalization rather than dangerous substring replacement.
- Do not silently assign 16:00 or any arbitrary time if the user has not specified a time.
- Represent an activity without an explicitly assigned time as an unscheduled task.
- Ask for clarification when a time expression is genuinely ambiguous.

Use deterministic Kotlin logic for time validation and Persian date conversion. AI may interpret natural language, but it must not bypass these validations.

